  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nMid-level clouds with bases around 15k feet (give or take) continue \nacross the eastern CWA but look to be exiting over the next few \nhours, also taking with it any sprinkles associated with them. Still \nexpect to see a lull in precip potential for the rest of the \nafternoon into early evening. Focus for shower/thunderstorm \ndevelopment later today will shift into eastern WY/CO into the NE \npanhandle northward into western SD. SPC REFS and HREF ensemble \npaintball output shows any potential activity moving into central SD \ngenerally between 02Z-05Z, then spreading eastward across the CWA \nduring the overnight hours. Areal coverage looks to diminish east of \nthe James River from 09Z to 12Z. Current PoPs generally show this \nidea where good agreement in the paintball output over central/north \ncentral SD gives 60-90% chances for precip, diminishing to 30 \npercent or less east of the James River after 09Z. MUCAPE overnight \ngenerally around 1000-1500 J/KG across central SD into the James \nvalley, with 30-40 kts effective shear. Marginal Risk continues for \nthe overnight hours, with main threats being winds up to around 60 \nmph and hail around quarter size. \n\nOn Sunday, a surface low will develop near the MT/ND border \nsouthward across western SD. A warm front will also extend eastward \nfrom this low across SD into southern MN. Surface dewpoints still \nprogged to reach the upper 60s to around 70 degrees across the \nregion, while cooling aloft occurs later in the day with the \napproach of a shortwave trough. Expecting scattered storms to \ndevelop by late afternoon near the surface low, with activity moving \neastward into the evening and overnight hours. Marginal Risk (level \n1 out of 5) for severe storms exists again on Sunday.\n\nSurface high pressure builds in across the area on Monday, with \nlower dewpoints dropping into the 50s for most areas. Much more \nstable air mass in place on Monday, so a dry forecast is in place \nfor the time being. \n\nPrecipitation chances (40-60%) return to the region Tuesday/Tuesday \nnight as shortwave energy moves northeast into the area. Low-level \nmoisture begins to increase once again with dewpoints forecast to \nreach the mid/upper 60s once again.",
